Feed a started A2DP source stream silence while its client is late (Y2Linux).

A started stream carried packets only while its client wrote audio. A client
that was late (a busy player, an underrun and its recovery, a pause that
keeps the PCM open) left the stream started with no packets at all, a state
phones never put headphones in: their mixer keeps writing, music or silence,
until the stream is suspended. AirPods Pro stayed silent after such a gap
until a new stream started (Y2Linux #96, F43, F69). The player feeds silence
itself since Beta 9.3.16, but that only holds while the player's own audio
thread runs on time, and it does not cover an underrun's Drop and Start.

Now the encoder thread fills in. Once a client has played, a stream that gets
no audio for 100 ms is fed silence at real time until audio arrives again.
The 100 ms it waited is made up by the bounded catch-up (patch 0009), so the
headphones' buffer returns to its level. Nothing changes for a client that
is on time. The end of playback (drain) and a closed PCM stop the stream as
before; a paused PCM now carries silence, as it stays started. Only A2DP
source streams are filled, never calls or capture.

The rule is in src/y2-filler.h (pure, tested on the host by
tests/test_bluetooth_filler.py). Checked against the real
io_poll_and_read_pcm() on the host with a scripted client: late for 600 ms,
the longest gap between packets is 100 ms (600 before) and 604 ms of audio
went out in those 600 ms; paused for 800 ms, 104 ms (800 before); after a
drain or a close no silence follows; a call's stream is untouched.

